# Netlify response headers (2026-11-03, his go on the blog speed fix).
# Photos had "max-age=0, must-revalidate", so every visit re-checked every image with the server.
# Browsers may now keep them for a day (fresh), and for up to a week after that may show the
# copy they have once while they fetch the new one in the background (stale-while-revalidate).
# So a photo replaced under the SAME filename can show its old version to a returning visitor:
# for up to a day, then for one more view any time within the following week. Replacement
# photos should get new filenames (the pipeline names new photos fresh). A deploy refreshes
# Netlify's own servers, not visitors' browsers.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/blog/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# Security headers on every response (2026-11-08, his go after the Codex site review). Standard,
# low-risk set: no MIME sniffing, no framing by other sites, referrer trimmed to the origin when
# leaving the site, and no camera/microphone/location access for any page or embed. A full
# Content-Security-Policy is not set: the pages use inline scripts and handlers, Google Fonts and
# YouTube embeds, so a CSP needs its own pass. HSTS is already sent by Netlify.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
